{"name":"Signpost Oak","hex":"#756657","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"signpost","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A post bearing a sign that gives information on directions.","A word or phrase within a clue that serves as an indicator, rather than being fodder."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To install signposts on.","To direct (somebody) to services, resources, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/signpost"},{"term":"oak","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Noakhali."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deciduous tree with distinctive deeply lobed leaves, acorns, and notably strong wood, typically of England and northeastern North America, included in genus Quercus.","The wood of the oak."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oak"}],"entries":[{"title":"Traffic sign","blocks":[{"text":"Traffic signs or road signs are signs erected at the side of or above roads to give instructions or provide information to road users. The earliest signs were simple wooden or stone milestones. Later, signs with directional arms were introduced, for example the fingerposts in the United Kingdom and their wooden counterparts in Saxony."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sturminster_Marshall%2C_new_signage_on_the_A350_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1741455.jpg/330px-Sturminster_Marshall%2C_new_signage_on_the_A350_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1741455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traffic_sign","wikidata":"Q170285"},{"title":"Oak","blocks":[{"text":"An oak is a hardwood tree or shrub in the genus Quercus of the beech family. They have spirally arranged leaves, often with lobed edges, and a nut called an acorn, borne within a cup. The genus is widely distributed in the Northern Hemisphere, with some 475 species, both deciduous and evergreen. Fossil oaks date back to the Middle Eocene. Molecular phylogeny shows that the genus is divided into Old World and New World clades, but many oak species hybridise freely, making the genus's history difficult to resolve."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Quercus_robur.jpg/330px-Quercus_robur.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oak","wikidata":"Q12004"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sturminster_Marshall%2C_new_signage_on_the_A350_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1741455.jpg/330px-Sturminster_Marshall%2C_new_signage_on_the_A350_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1741455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sturminster_Marshall%2C_new_signage_on_the_A350_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1741455.jpg/330px-Sturminster_Marshall%2C_new_signage_on_the_A350_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1741455.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fingerposts and other road signage in the English village of Sturminster Marshall, near Poole","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASturminster_Marshall%2C_new_signage_on_the_A350_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1741455.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Signpost_on_Warrego_Highway_-Australia-1Aug2009.jpg/500px-Signpost_on_Warrego_Highway_-Australia-1Aug2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Signpost_on_Warrego_Highway_-Australia-1Aug2009.jpg/1280px-Signpost_on_Warrego_Highway_-Australia-1Aug2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Signpost_on_Warrego_Highway_-Australia-1Aug2009.jpg/500px-Signpost_on_Warrego_Highway_-Australia-1Aug2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thum